2026-09-14 02:20 slice 35: record op_eq recursion, op_neq alignment, implicit ctor args, interp formats
- record_types crashed with stack exhaustion: `l == null` inside the synthesized
  op_eq dispatched back into op_eq. ngen's TryOpOverloadNg now keeps comparisons
  against the null literal as reference compares (gen0's null_cmp rule). Also
  renamed op_ne -> op_neq across the parser's operator naming, the record
  template and the dispatch table, matching gen0's spelling.
- implicit_ctor_argument: a bool/integer/char/enum call argument whose
  parameter is a declared class now rewrites to `new Target(arg)` when a
  one-argument ctor fits (gen0 wrap_implicit_ctor_arg); otherwise the gen0
  "no matching constructor" diagnostic fires. ClassTyOf strips generic
  arguments (`GBox<bool>` -> `GBox`, erasure), and the target must be a
  declared class so the wrap never recurses into open type parameters.
- interpolation format specs: the lexer's hole scanner treats a top-level ':'
  as the spec separator (paren depth guards ternaries) and splices
  `expr.ToString("spec")`; ngen maps specs to printf patterns exactly like
  gen0's interp_format_to_printf (D/X/F/E/G/custom 0#; D/X ignored on double
  receivers) and renders through the new _zan_rt_fmt runtime helper
  (two-pass snprintf into a str_alloc'd, header-stamped heap string; the
  value travels on the stack per AAPCS64 variadic rules).
- ScalarToStringTy answers "int" for IntLit receivers.
- bootstrap fixed point 6,506,504 bytes; kernels 27/27; EH stress green;
  sweep 89 -> 95 of 537 (record_types, implicit_ctor_argument,
  cs_b10_interp_format + others; no regressions).
